      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
          "content": "The aim of this retrospective study was to determine the predictors of diffuse in-stent restenosis (ISR) among the lesions causing the first ISR by intravascular ultrasound (IVUS) studies."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Although some predictors of diffuse ISR have been reported, parameters on IVUS relating to diffuse ISR are not well characterized."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We classified 52 ISR lesions that had undergone successful stent implantation and led to restenosis into two types--focal and diffuse ISR--using quantitative coronary angiography. Restenosis was defined as > or =50% diameter stenosis, and diffuse ISR as lesion length > or =10 mm at follow-up. The remodeling index (RI) was defined as the vessel area at the target lesion divided by that of averaged reference segments."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "There were no significant differences in patient, angiographic, and procedural characteristics between the focal (n = 25) and diffuse (n = 27) ISR groups. Baseline RI was significantly greater in the diffuse ISR group (1.03 +/- 0.18 vs. 0.88 +/- 0.24, p = 0.0159). Negative remodeling, defined as RI <0.9, was detected in 60% of the focal ISR group and in only 26% of the diffuse ISR group. By logistic regression analysis, baseline RI was the only independent predictor of diffuse ISR (p = 0.0341). Moreover, volumetric analyses revealed that lesions developing into diffuse ISR had less capacity to compensate for further plaque growth."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Among the first ISR lesions, baseline positive remodeling was the most powerful predictor of diffuse ISR. Measuring pre-interventional arterial remodeling patterns by IVUS may be helpful to stratify lesions at higher risk."
        }
      ]},
